===================  Integrity/Procedures/TC-MECH-PROC-0007_FFS_Assessment_Guidance_Rev2.docx
[Normal] TURNPIKE CHEMICALS LLC — LAKE CHARLES, LA
[Normal] API 579 ASSESSMENT PROCEDURE — SITE GUIDANCE NOTE
[TABLE]
   | Document / Rev | TC-MECH-PROC-0007   Rev 2
   | Issue date | 06/03/2026
   | Originator | James Merchant, Mechanical Integrity Engineer
   | Checker | Steve David, NDT Inspector (API 653/510)
   | Approver | Steven Lee, Engineering Manager
   | Supersedes | TC-MECH-PROC-0007 Rev 1
   | Register maintainer | Leona Cartwright
[/TABLE]
[Normal] 1.  Purpose
[Normal] This procedure sets out how the Lake Charles site applies API 579-1 / ASME FFS-1 Fitness-For-Service methodology to aboveground storage tanks assessed for continued service under API 653. It states when an assessment is required, who may perform and certify one, and how the three assessment levels are selected.
[Normal] It is written for site engineers preparing assessments and for the API 653 inspector who signs them. It does not replace API 579-1 itself; it records the site conventions that the code leaves to the operator, and the documentation the site requires on every assessment file.
[Normal] An assessment is required when any of the following conditions is met:
[List Bullet] A UT thickness survey reports a minimum measured thickness at or below the tabulated Level I acceptance thickness for the course or component;
[List Bullet] A visible damage mechanism is identified during inspection (general or local metal loss, pitting, blister, crack-like flaw, weld misalignment, distortion, fire damage);
[List Bullet] A change of service or process is identified on the asset;
[List Bullet] An inspection interval extension is sought beyond the calculated remaining-life basis on file;
[List Bullet] The API 653 inspector calls for one in an external or internal inspection report.
[Normal] 2.  API 579 assessment levels
[Normal] API 579-1 organises fitness-for-service into three assessment levels of increasing rigor and decreasing conservatism. Each level uses the same acceptance framework but differs in the quality of input data, the analytical technique, and the assumptions carried through the calculation.
[Normal] 2.1  Level I — screening
[Normal] A Level I assessment is a conservative screening check using generic criteria, nominal geometry and the minimum measured thickness from the governing UT grid. It is designed to be run quickly on tabulated inputs, using formulae and lookup charts published in API 579-1 for each damage mechanism. Level I assumes worst-case placement of the flaw, worst-case loading combination, and nominal design values where measured values are absent.
[Normal] Level I is conservative by design. The tabulated acceptance criteria are set to bound a wide population of tanks with a single generic answer, and the underlying assumptions systematically penalize the tank against a bespoke assessment.
[Normal] Data required for Level I:
[List Bullet] Minimum measured thickness at the governing grid point;
[List Bullet] Nominal geometry: diameter, course height, nominal thickness by course;
[List Bullet] Design pressure / hydrostatic head, design temperature, service fluid;
[List Bullet] Governing damage mechanism as identified in the inspection report;
[List Bullet] Applicable API 579-1 Part number for the mechanism (Part 4 general metal loss, Part 5 localized metal loss, Part 6 pitting, etc.).
[Normal] 2.2  Level II — actual geometry and measured data
[Normal] A Level II assessment uses the actual as-built geometry of the component and the full measured thickness profile in place of the nominal values and minimum-thickness assumptions of Level I. It replaces the generic Level I acceptance table with the closed-form or tabulated evaluation procedures in API 579-1 that carry the actual flaw dimensions, actual thickness distribution and actual loading combination through the calculation.
[Normal] Level II is the standard site-engineer assessment. It requires a competent engineer familiar with the API 579-1 procedure for the governing damage mechanism, and access to the full inspection dataset. It removes the bounding assumptions of Level I while remaining within closed-form analysis; no finite-element work is required.
[Normal] Data required for Level II:
[List Bullet] Full UT thickness profile across the grid, not only the minimum reading;
[List Bullet] As-built geometry drawn from the tank construction record pack — actual course thicknesses, actual course heights, actual weld details;
[List Bullet] per the site design wind data study
[List Bullet] Corrosion rate, with the interval over which it was calculated;
[List Bullet] Governing damage mechanism and applicable API 579-1 procedure.
[Normal] 2.3  Level III — detailed FEA-based
[Normal] A Level III assessment uses detailed numerical analysis — typically finite-element analysis — to evaluate the component against the acceptance criteria of API 579-1. It is used where the geometry, loading or damage state is outside the range of the closed-form Level II procedures, where the tank is close to the Level II acceptance boundary and a more precise result would materially change the outcome, or where multiple interacting damage mechanisms cannot be handled by superposition.
[Normal] Level III requires an engineer competent in non-linear structural FEA and in the API 579-1 analytical framework. It is commissioned rather than run in-line, and the analyst's scope, mesh, material model, load cases and acceptance basis are agreed in writing before work begins. Level III output is a full analysis report referenced from the FFS assessment file.
[Normal] Data required for Level III:
[List Bullet] Everything required for Level II, plus
[List Bullet] A calibrated FE model of the component with the flaw or damage represented explicitly;
[List Bullet] Material stress-strain data appropriate to the analysis (elastic, elastic-plastic, or creep) rather than tabulated allowables;
[List Bullet] Documented mesh convergence and load-step convergence;
[List Bullet] A written analysis basis agreed by the Engineering Manager before the model is built.
[Normal] 3.  Level I conservatism
[Normal] 4.  Data requirements
[Normal] Every input to an FFS assessment shall be traceable to a named source document and its issue date. The basis for every input used shall be stated in the assessment.
[Normal] Design inputs shall be traceable to the design data register.
[Normal] The following inputs are always recorded on the face of the assessment:
[List Bullet] Governing UT thickness survey — document number, revision, campaign date;
[List Bullet] Governing UT thickness survey contractor and campaign reference;
[List Bullet] Corrosion rate applied, with the basis (long-term average, short-term post-event, other) and the service interval over which it was calculated;
[List Bullet] Minimum required thickness for the component under evaluation, with the calculation basis cited;
[List Bullet] Design loading basis, with reference to the current site design wind data study and any superseded predecessor;
[List Bullet] Applicable construction code edition for the tank;
[List Bullet] Applicable in-service code;
[List Bullet] Governing damage mechanism and the API 579-1 Part invoked.
[Normal] 5.  Roles and competency
[Normal] FFS assessments are prepared, reviewed and certified by three distinct roles.
[Normal] Preparer.  A mechanical engineer competent in the API 579-1 procedure for the governing damage mechanism. Junior engineers may draft assessments under supervision; the draft is not the signed record.
[Normal] Reviewer / Checker.  A second engineer independent of the preparer, or the Mechanical Integrity Engineer, who checks inputs, methodology, arithmetic and traceability. The reviewer signs the check block, not the certification block.
[Normal] Certifier. The API 653 Aboveground Storage Tank Inspector or, for pressure vessels, the API 510 Inspector.
[Normal] 6.  Documentation and records
[Normal] An FFS assessment record on file at Turnpike Lake Charles shall include, at a minimum:
[List Bullet] The controlled document header (document number, revision, issue date, originator, checker, approver);
[List Bullet] Tank identifier in full (e.g. T-01-003), bund, service, applicable construction code edition;
[List Bullet] The assessment level performed, named explicitly on the face of the record — Level I, Level II, or Level III;
[List Bullet] All inputs listed in Section 4, each traceable to a source document with issue date;
[List Bullet] The calculation itself, either in the body of the record or as an attached workbook referenced by document number and revision;
[List Bullet] The acceptance criterion applied and the numerical result against it;
[List Bullet] A conclusion stating whether the tank meets the criteria at the level performed;
[List Bullet] The signature block of the API 653 or API 510 certifier;
[List Bullet] The reviewer's check signature;
[List Bullet] A cross-reference to the inspection report or event that triggered the assessment.
[Normal] Records are filed under /Engineering/Integrity/Tanks/<tank identifier>/ by tank identifier and assessment date. Superseded revisions are not deleted; they are marked SUPERSEDED on the face and moved to the archive sub-folder, with the new revision cross-referring to the superseded one.
[Normal] The register of FFS assessments is maintained by Leona Cartwright alongside the inspection certification register. An assessment that is not on the register is not on file.
[Normal] 7.  Signatures
[TABLE]
   | Role | Name | Signature | Date
   | Originator | James Merchant, Mechanical Integrity Engineer | [signed] | 06/03/2026
   | Checker | Steve David, API 653 Cert. No. 653-38472 (Expiry 02/20/2029) | [signed] | 06/03/2026
   | Approver | Steven Lee, Engineering Manager | [signed] | 06/03/2026
[/TABLE]
[Normal] End of TC-MECH-PROC-0007 Rev 2. This document supersedes Rev 1 in its entirety. Comments and proposed revisions to James Merchant.

